WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.655
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.655 --> 00:00:10.940
I'm Jules van Binsbergen, the Nippon Life
Professor in Finance at the Wharton School

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.343
at the University of Pennsylvania.

00:00:12.343 --> 00:00:15.247
&gt;&gt; And I'm Jonathan Berk,
the AP Giannini Professor of Finance at

00:00:15.247 --> 00:00:17.903
the Graduate School of Business
at Stanford University.

00:00:17.903 --> 00:00:22.328
And we hope you'll join us on a journey
where we explore the science and

00:00:22.328 --> 00:00:25.928
strategy of better decision-making
on All Else Equal,

00:00:25.928 --> 00:00:31.261
a new podcast from the Stanford Graduate
School of Business and the Wharton School.

00:00:31.261 --> 00:00:34.493
&gt;&gt; Together we have been teaching and
studying business decision-making for

00:00:34.493 --> 00:00:35.260
over 40 years.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.503
And in that time, we've learned about
the most common mistakes that people make.

00:00:39.503 --> 00:00:42.946
And we've also learned a thing or
two about how to explain those mistakes to

00:00:42.946 --> 00:00:46.678
decision makers, particularly after
having made them repeatedly ourselves.

00:00:46.678 --> 00:00:50.379
&gt;&gt; In this podcast series, we'll look
at the ways people make mistakes,

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:52.546
and how we can learn from their mistakes.

00:00:52.546 --> 00:00:55.416
We'll talk to business leaders about
choices they have had to make.

00:00:55.416 --> 00:00:59.688
And we'll show you a framework you can
use to become a better decision maker,

00:00:59.688 --> 00:01:02.727
not just on the job, but
in your personal life as well.

00:01:02.727 --> 00:01:06.459
&gt;&gt; Over the years, one of the things we
have found is that abstract theoretical

00:01:06.459 --> 00:01:08.692
concepts just don't resonate with people.

00:01:08.692 --> 00:01:11.161
What does resonate are concrete examples,
and

00:01:11.161 --> 00:01:14.562
that insight is the basis of
the case method in business schools.

00:01:14.562 --> 00:01:17.651
By explaining a general principle in
the context of an interesting example,

00:01:17.651 --> 00:01:20.140
the ideas become interesting and
fun to talk about.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.220
It is also how people remember ideas.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.964
There's lots of research that shows that
concreteness is key to how the brain

00:01:25.964 --> 00:01:26.724
stores ideas.

00:01:26.724 --> 00:01:31.258
&gt;&gt; In each podcast, we'll discuss
specific interesting examples.

00:01:31.258 --> 00:01:35.915
And we'll invite successful leaders to
talk about their personal experience

00:01:35.915 --> 00:01:38.663
making the mistake,
why it was so costly, and

00:01:38.663 --> 00:01:41.075
how they try to avoid them in the future.

00:01:41.075 --> 00:01:42.831
&gt;&gt; And so in our first few episodes,

00:01:42.831 --> 00:01:47.167
we will explore a number of business
decision-making situations where ignoring

00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:50.644
the reaction of the world around
you leads to the wrong decision.

00:01:50.644 --> 00:01:54.492
This is why we've decided to
call the podcast All Else Equal.

00:01:54.492 --> 00:01:58.230
&gt;&gt; When you make a decision,
the world around you reacts.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.811
It's not just the world
is constantly changing,

00:02:00.811 --> 00:02:03.829
it is the world changes because
of the decisions you make.

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:08.083
&gt;&gt; This type of all else equal
thinking is the common thread through

00:02:08.083 --> 00:02:09.511
the podcast series.

00:02:09.511 --> 00:02:11.590
Make sure to catch each
episode by following or

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.429
subscribing to our show wherever
you listen to your podcasts.

00:02:14.429 --> 00:02:19.049
For more information and episodes,
visit allelseequalpodcast.com.

00:02:19.049 --> 00:02:27.460
[MUSIC]

